Abstract

The Geospatial Multi‑Physics Stratum Simulator (GMSS) is a unified computational framework designed to model coupled astrophysical, geomechanical, multiphase fluid, fracture‑mechanical, and biogeochemical processes within Earth’s subsurface. GMSS integrates dynamic gravitational forcing derived from astrophysical datasets, visco‑elasto‑plastic geomechanics, multiphase flow of water and methane, Mohr‑Coulomb fracture mechanics, and microbial reaction‑diffusion systems. All state transitions are computed within a homomorphically encrypted environment and validated using zero‑knowledge proofs, producing a cryptographically governed record of planetary evolution. GMSS represents a novel class of multi‑domain Earth system simulator with potential implications for geophysics, computational trust, and planetary science.

1. Introduction

Earth system simulations traditionally isolate physical domains such as geomechanics, hydrology, fracture propagation, and biogeochemistry. This separation limits the ability to capture emergent phenomena arising from deep coupling between physical, chemical, and biological processes. GMSS proposes a unified architecture that integrates these domains and introduces cryptographic verification to ensure the integrity of simulation outputs.

The primary objectives of GMSS are:

To incorporate astrophysical forcing into subsurface physics.

To unify geomechanical, fluid, fracture, and biogeochemical processes.

To enforce cryptographic validation of all state transitions.

To produce tamper‑proof, physically consistent planetary evolution histories.

2. System Architecture

2.1 Core Components

Astrophysical Forcing Module: Computes gravitational field variations using stellar‑motion datasets.

Encrypted Compute Kernel: Executes physics calculations under homomorphic encryption.

Zero‑Knowledge Verification Layer: Validates each timestep using ZK‑Snark proofs.

Geomechanical Solver: Implements Biot consolidation for coupled pressure–deformation behavior.

Multiphase Flow Engine: Models water–methane transport using Darcy multiphase flow.

Fracture Mechanics Engine: Evaluates failure using Mohr‑Coulomb yield criterion.

Biogeochemical Engine: Simulates microbial and chemical reaction‑diffusion processes.

Global Ledger: Stores validated states in an immutable sequence.

